WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
I wanted to actually start uh with a challenge,&nbsp;
a little puzzle. People are probably familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:12.000
with the tetrahedron, and here's a square based&nbsp;
pyramid, so the triangular faces are both the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
on these shapes. The challenge for you, and we'll
perhaps solve it at the end of the video, is if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.080
put these two shapes together tell me how many&nbsp;
faces there are on the shape that I would make

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.960
by fusing two of the triangular faces together.&nbsp;
Okay, there's your challenge. You might think, oh,&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.200
it doesn't sound terribly difficult for us&nbsp;
Numberphile- so but we'll look at that at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.240
the end. What I wanted to talk about in this video&nbsp;
was an interesting thing I discovered working with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.640
a sculptor that lives nearby my house here;&nbsp;
he's very famous actually, Conrad Shawross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:49.680
and he loves using mathematical shapes in the&nbsp;
sculptures that he makes. And one of the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:56.240
challenges he faced, he realised that using a bit&nbsp;
of mathematics actually cut down the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:02.640
work that he had to do as a sculptor. So he's been&nbsp;
really obsessed with this shape the tetrahedron;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.920
he sort of thought this was like one of the&nbsp;
great building blocks of nature, and indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:15.920
he's right. The ancient Greeks of course thought&nbsp;
the platonic solids were the kind of atoms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:22.480
made up the universe. So at that time we thought&nbsp;
earth, wind, fire and water were the atoms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:28.000
the universe; and each of those would have a&nbsp;
shape. Earth, that was the shape of the cube;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.560
water, the icosahedron, made out of 20 equilateral&nbsp;
triangles; air was the octahedron made out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.840
eight equilateral triangles; and fire was this&nbsp;
shape here, the tetrahedron, the kind of spikiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.560
of the shapes. Of course we've got one platonic&nbsp;
solid left over which is the dodecahedron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.560
and Plato left that to be the shape of the&nbsp;
universe, which is kind of interesting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.600
But Conrad Shawcross was- was interested&nbsp;
as a sculptor in, yeah, well what about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:02.720
taking this atomic shape the tetrahedron and and&nbsp;
seeing what he can build out of it. So one of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:08.880
very first projects, he built 2,000 tetrahedrons&nbsp;
and set himself the task of trying to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.240
what he could build out of these tetrahedrons.&nbsp;
And actually he had a deadline and suddenly the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.200
deadline looms and he's got to try and find how&nbsp;
to put these 2,000 tetrahedron together; but then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:24.400
he talked to a mathematician who said, well you&nbsp;
realise you don't actually have 2,000 objects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.040
you've got to find how to put them together? In&nbsp;
fact you have a much smaller number of building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.760
blocks. Because if you put two tetrahedron&nbsp;
together for example there's really only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.560
one shape you can make out of that, there isn't&nbsp;
another way I can put these tetrahedron together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.480
and get a different sort of shape. So&nbsp;
already actually you've halved the problem;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.840
and and this is the power of symmetry, you&nbsp;
know, and that's my area of expertise, symmetry.&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.720
Symmetry often is a clever way of cutting down a&nbsp;
problem which at first sight seems like there are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:01.520
you know, 2,000 different things you've got to&nbsp;
put together. But then he realised, no actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.640
you can halve that. There are only 1,000 objects&nbsp;
that you need to consider, I've just got to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.000
these together. But the mathematician said, no&nbsp;
in fact you can cut it down even more because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.160
if I have three tetrahedron there's only one&nbsp;
way I can put three tetrahedron together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.680
So here are my little paper tetrahedron I made&nbsp;
earlier for you. Here's our two put together;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:30.160
but actually there's only one way I can attach&nbsp;
a third object, and what I make is this kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:35.920
weird shape almost little like a boat isn't it?&nbsp;
So you might say, okay well that's one way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.400
put the tetrahedron together but maybe I can&nbsp;
fuse this on a different edge - and look I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.720
got something different. But of course the point&nbsp;
is that symmetry means that I can turn this and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.840
I've always got that little boat structure. So it&nbsp;
doesn't matter how many different ways I try, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:56.400
symmetry means I'll always get the same object.
So Conrad used this as a shortcut in a way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:01.280
the symmetry of how you put these together,&nbsp;
to say I don't need to consider 2,000 I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.600
only need to consider a third of that and&nbsp;
use this as my basic building block; and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.400
that's what he used to make some of his uh first&nbsp;
interesting structures&nbsp;out of this tetrahedron.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:14.800
(Brady: But if you go up to four&nbsp;then it-&nbsp;
then you lose it? Four you can&nbsp;start-)

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.760
Yeah then you start to um have uh different&nbsp;
directions that you're coming out, and so this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:25.680
really the basic building block for his sculptures&nbsp;
is this particular one here - but then there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.080
new decisions you've got to make. And what&nbsp;
he found was there were interesting tendrils;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.200
quite unexpected shapes that emerged out&nbsp;
of this. If you look at Conrad's work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:42.240
you see just amazing kind of weird directions that&nbsp;
he's been taken on by this particular object. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.840
recently he's discovered a kind of new sort of&nbsp;
shape which I actually had not been aware of;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.320
because one of the interesting thing is with&nbsp;
platonic solids, which of them can you pack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.480
and fill space with? A cube? Clearly you can&nbsp;
stack cubes and and fill the whole of space, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:04.560
do any of the other objects actually fill&nbsp;
space? You can take a a slightly non-symmetrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:11.920
tetrahedron, squash it a bit and fill space with&nbsp;
that. But with the actual symmetrical tetrahedron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.960
if you try and fill space it doesn't work, and&nbsp;
there's always kind of a little bit left over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.520
And Conrad's made this very interesting&nbsp;
shape that looks like it's trying to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.720
an icosahedron, it's sort of building up lots&nbsp;
of triangles, but they don't quite fit together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.960
and there's kind of an interesting gap. I&nbsp;
think that's very interesting, an artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:36.000
using these shapes to sort of discover very&nbsp;a
asymmetrical shape made out of these tetrahedrons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.480
It looks like it's trying to be something&nbsp;
symmetrical but doesn't quite work, it's kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:46.400
interesting, the sweet spot between something that&nbsp;
is trying to be perfect but it isn't quite. (Does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.800
that tetrahedral boat have a name?) - It really&nbsp;
should do doesn't it? I mean- but now you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:56.000
challenged me with that I don't know what that&nbsp;
would be called - perhaps people in the comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.600
down below, either we can christen it, give it a&nbsp;
name, or maybe somebody does know&nbsp;a name for that.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.240
Perhaps it could be called the&nbsp;Shawcross shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:06.080
or something.
- (It is boat-y.)

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.880
It is a little bit boat-y.&nbsp;
So I think it's interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.240
how you can use symmetry very often to to&nbsp;
cut down the hard work you've got to do;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.760
and and very often in when I'm looking&nbsp;
at a particular mathematical problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.680
sometimes you're looking at an equation um&nbsp;
and it can be solved in many different ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:29.760
uh but spotting that actually all of the solutions&nbsp;
are kind of symmetrical versions of each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.760
is often a way of cutting down the work. You&nbsp;
just have to find one solution and then use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:40.800
symmetry to find all of the others. So that's a&nbsp;
very common trick in mathematics and in science.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.760
But it was intriguing, it was the first time&nbsp;
I'd seen it used by a sculptor as a- you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:51.440
oh yeah use the symmetry and cut down the work.
- (What work was&nbsp;he cutting down Professor?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:52.400
Was it- was it- was)

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:54.800
(he having to cast less pieces&nbsp;
or something like&nbsp;that?)&nbsp;

00:06:54.800 --> 00:07:02.320
- Well he'd actually made all of these 2,000&nbsp;
cubes out of wood; but what he'd cut down was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:06.160
just- what he needed to put together was these&nbsp;
three and then they were the building blocks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.480
from which everything else- you know, there was&nbsp;
nothing new that would emerge if he hadn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:15.280
done that. At first I think there may be many more&nbsp;
possibilities but the symmetry showed, no, all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:19.280
these are actually examples of the same thing. So&nbsp;
maybe we should come back to our little challenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.560
because that was about putting shapes together and&nbsp;
understanding what shape emerges by putting two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:33.440
together. So at first sight you may say this is&nbsp;
pretty obvious - this shape has 5 faces, this one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:41.920
has 4 faces, when I put these together I lose two&nbsp;
faces. So 5 plus 4 is 9, so I should get 7 faces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:47.600
So what's the big uh problem? But&nbsp;
something rather curious happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:53.520
because when I put these together I lose two&nbsp;
more faces! Because look at this - these two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:08:02.400
line up, and so these two actually become one&nbsp;
face, so this object actually has 5 faces: 1, 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:08.720
3, 4, 5 faces.
So that's a little bit

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.680
of a surprise that comes out of that. I hope you&nbsp;
perhaps experienced an 'oh, that's unexpected'.&nbsp;

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:22.480
And what's curious about this is this was set in&nbsp;
one of the SAT exams uh in America, just to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:28.960
how good you are at conceptual visualisation. And&nbsp;
they didn't realise that um- this kind of rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:37.120
curious answer. So the the people who said 7
got the score and those who said 5 um were given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.160
no marks at all, but actually it turns out&nbsp;
rather curiously that when you put these together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:48.240
um this shape actually only has 5 faces. In&nbsp;
this shape you've got um 2 faces on this side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:54.080
and 2 that faces on this side that fuse. So so&nbsp;
actually this - which you might have first thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.080 --> 00:09:00.400
counted as uh 2 different faces - you've&nbsp;
got 1, 2, 3, the square on the bottom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.440
and the last triangle there, 5.
- (Totally got me.)

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.080
I&nbsp;know!
- (I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:10.480
have got the mark in the&nbsp;SATs though.)
- Exactly! That was what was so crazy.&nbsp;

